1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,835
Hello and welcome to The Owl's Lessons!

2
00:00:03,535 --> 00:00:07,965
Last time, you travelled back in time with the history of backgammon.

3
00:00:09,263 --> 00:00:16,565
Today is the last lesson of the season: the player's vocabulary. Blot, prime, gammon, doubling cube…

4
00:00:16,265 --> 00:00:21,117
By the end, you'll talk backgammon like a club regular! Let's go!

5
00:00:24,097 --> 00:00:28,931
Let's start with the board. Each of the twenty-four triangles is called a point.

6
00:00:30,776 --> 00:00:34,707
The distance from one point to the next is called a pip.

7
00:00:34,407 --> 00:00:37,884
A checker that moves five points moves five pips.

8
00:00:38,561 --> 00:00:41,761
In the middle, the central strip is called the bar.

9
00:00:41,461 --> 00:00:47,107
Your points one to six are your home board, and your points seven to twelve, your outer board.

10
00:00:51,913 --> 00:00:57,452
A single checker on a point is called a blot. It's fragile: your opponent can hit it.

11
00:00:58,374 --> 00:01:04,242
With at least two checkers, you make a point. It's yours: your opponent can no longer land there.

12
00:01:05,334 --> 00:01:09,544
And a point you hold in your opponent's home board is called an anchor.

13
00:01:09,244 --> 00:01:11,637
It's a safe haven, deep in their camp.

14
00:01:15,099 --> 00:01:21,612
To slow your opponent down, you block. Your seven-point, right next to the bar, is called the bar point.

15
00:01:22,550 --> 00:01:25,581
Several made points in a row form a prime.

16
00:01:25,281 --> 00:01:30,264
With six points in a row, the prime is complete: nobody can get past it!

17
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:36,134
And a checker placed a few pips away from a point you want to make is called a builder.

18
00:01:35,834 --> 00:01:37,699
It prepares your next point.

19
00:01:40,422 --> 00:01:45,917
When your checker lands on an opponent's blot, you hit it: the hit checker goes to the bar.

20
00:01:47,232 --> 00:01:53,354
That checker must then enter from the bar into its opponent's home board, before anything else can be played.

21
00:01:55,548 --> 00:02:01,294
A blot six pips away or less can be hit with a single die: that's a direct shot.

22
00:02:00,994 --> 00:02:06,945
Further away, you have to add both dice: that's an indirect shot, much less likely.

23
00:02:09,365 --> 00:02:14,207
When no checker can pass the opponent's checkers anymore, the game becomes a race.

24
00:02:15,701 --> 00:02:21,524
To know who's ahead, you do a pip count: the total distance your checkers still have to travel.

25
00:02:21,224 --> 00:02:25,186
At the start, it's one hundred and sixty-seven for each player.

26
00:02:27,081 --> 00:02:31,719
When all fifteen of your checkers are in your home board, you can bear them off.

27
00:02:31,419 --> 00:02:34,702
The first player to bear off all of them wins the game.

28
00:02:37,288 --> 00:02:44,231
Here is the doubling cube, the big die marked two, four, eight, sixteen, thirty-two and sixty-four.

29
00:02:43,931 --> 00:02:46,646
Players often just call it the cube.

30
00:02:48,383 --> 00:02:53,583
Before rolling the dice, you can double: that means offering to double the value of the game.

31
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:59,450
Your opponent then chooses. Either they take, and the game goes on, worth twice as much.

32
00:02:59,150 --> 00:03:03,847
Or they pass, and they lose the game straight away, at its current value.

33
00:03:05,747 --> 00:03:10,218
Winning normally is a single game: it counts for its basic value.

34
00:03:11,211 --> 00:03:16,464
If the loser hasn't borne off any checker, it's a gammon: the win counts double.

35
00:03:17,017 --> 00:03:24,900
And if they also still have a checker on the bar or in the winner's home board, it's a backgammon: the win counts triple!

36
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:28,221
So the game is named after the worst possible defeat.

37
00:03:30,834 --> 00:03:39,658
In clubs and tournaments, people play matches: a series of games to a number of points set in advance, for example five or seven points.

38
00:03:40,492 --> 00:03:47,364
When a player gets to one point from victory, the next game is played without the doubling cube: that's the Crawford rule.

39
00:03:47,808 --> 00:03:50,988
The very first move of the game is called the opening.

40
00:03:50,688 --> 00:03:54,722
There are only fifteen of them, and you discovered them in lesson two.

41
00:03:55,168 --> 00:03:56,493
And the chouette?

42
00:03:56,193 --> 00:04:02,762
It's backgammon for three or more players: the box plays alone against the whole team, led by a captain.

43
00:04:02,462 --> 00:04:05,233
You can guess why it's my favourite game!

44
00:04:07,731 --> 00:04:13,842
And there you go! Point, blot, anchor, prime, pip count, doubling cube, gammon…

45
00:04:13,542 --> 00:04:17,299
you now know the essential words of the player.

46
00:04:17,695 --> 00:04:22,213
To find them again at any time, open the backgammon glossary on our website.

47
00:04:22,414 --> 00:04:25,611
This lesson ends season one of The Owl's Lessons.

48
00:04:25,311 --> 00:04:30,282
Thank you for following it with me, from the very first rule to this last word!

49
00:04:31,294 --> 00:04:35,076
Season two is coming soon, with new lessons to go further.

50
00:04:35,676 --> 00:04:43,675
In the meantime, play backgammon for free against other players or against the computer, in your browser, on Android or on Windows.

51
00:04:44,196 --> 00:04:48,358
Subscribe so you don't miss what's next. See you soon around the board!

